{{define "pre-content"}}
  <link href="/static/frontend/about/about.min.css?version={{.AppVersionLabel}}" rel="stylesheet">
{{end}}

{{define "main"}}
  <main class="go-Container" id="main-content">
    <div class="about-Wrapper">
      <aside class="LeftNav-sidebar">
        {{/* Left nav generated here. */}}
        <nav class="LeftNav" data-hydrate="true"></nav>
      </aside>
      <div class="go-Content about-Content">
        <h1 data-test-id="about-heading">About pkg.lyuba.dev</h1>
        <p>
          pkg.lyuba.dev is the package site of the
          <a href="https://github.com/GlazKrovi/lyuba">Lyuba</a> language.
          It only lists modules that contain <code>.lyu</code> files; pure Go modules are left out.
          It is a fork of <a href="https://go.googlesource.com/pkgsite">pkgsite</a>, the software behind pkg.go.dev,
          and is an independent project, not affiliated with Google or the Go project.
        </p>

        <h2 id="adding-a-package">Adding a package</h2>
        <p>
          Data for the site is downloaded from the Go module proxy and index
          (<a href="https://proxy.golang.org/">proxy.golang.org</a>,
          <a href="https://index.golang.org/index">index.golang.org</a>), which Lyuba modules use like any module.
          The index is read regularly for new versions, and those with <code>.lyu</code> files are added.
          If you don’t see your package, visit its page, for example
          <code>https://pkg.lyuba.dev/example.com/my/module</code>, and click the “Request” button.
          Publishing a version with <code>lyuba publish</code> also makes it available to the proxy.
        </p>

        <h2 id="removing-a-package">Removing a package</h2>
        <p>
          To hide versions of a module from pkg.lyuba.dev and from the <code>go</code> command,
          retract them: add a <code>retract</code> directive to your go.mod file and publish a new version.
          See the <a href="https://go.dev/ref/mod#go-mod-file-retract">modules reference</a> for details.
          Published versions cannot be reused or modified, retracted ones included.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you cannot publish a new version, or want to hide the documentation of all versions,
          <a href="https://github.com/GlazKrovi/pkgsite/issues">open an issue</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="documentation">Documentation</h2>
        <p>
          Documentation is generated from the <code>.lyu</code> sources of each module version,
          downloaded from the proxy. The <code>.go</code> files generated next to them are ignored.
          Doc comments are the comments right above a declaration; the first sentence of the package comment
          is shown in search results, so make it a good summary of the package.
        </p>

        <h3 id="build-context">Build Context</h3>
        <p>
          Lyuba packages are rendered the same way for every operating system and architecture,
          unless the module has platform-specific files. When a package exists for only one
          <code>GOOS/GOARCH</code> pair, such as <code>linux/amd64</code>, that pair is displayed
          at the upper right of the documentation. When it differs between pairs, a dropdown lets you choose.
        </p>

        <h3 id="source-links">Source Links</h3>
        <p>
          When pkg.lyuba.dev can locate a package’s source, symbols in the documentation link to their definition.
          If yours are not linked, make sure the module path points to a known code host such as GitHub,
          or <a href="https://github.com/GlazKrovi/pkgsite/issues">open an issue</a> to have your host added.
        </p>

        <h2 id="best-practices">Best practices</h2>
        <p>
          Each module page shows the following details, which help consumers trust a module:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <b>Has <code>go.mod</code> file</b>.
              A Lyuba module is a Go module: a tree of source files with a go.mod file at its root.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <b>Redistributable license</b>.
              Redistributable licenses place minimal restrictions on how software can be used, modified and redistributed.
              See the <a href="/license-policy">license policy</a>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <b>Tagged version</b>.
              Dependency resolution prioritizes tagged versions; modules with tags give importers more predictable builds.
              See <a href="https://semver.org">semver.org</a>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <b>Stable version</b>.
              Projects at v0 are assumed to be experimental. From v1 on, breaking changes belong in a new major version.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="keyboard-shortcuts">Keyboard Shortcuts</h2>
        <p>
          There are keyboard shortcuts for navigating package documentation pages.
          Type ‘?’ on a package page for help.
        </p>

        <h2 id="bookmarklet">Bookmarklet</h2>
        <p>
          The bookmarklet navigates from pages on source code hosts, such as GitHub, to the package documentation.
          To install it, drag the following link to your bookmark bar:
          <a href="javascript:(function(){ const pathRegex = window.location.pathname.match(/([^\/]+)(?:\/([^\/]+))?/); const host = window.location.hostname; if (pathRegex) { window.location='https://pkg.lyuba.dev/'+host+'/'+pathRegex[0]; } else { alert('There was an error navigating to pkg.lyuba.dev!'); } })()">Lyuba Doc</a>
        </p>

        <h2 id="license-policy">License policy</h2>
        <p>
          Information for a given package or module may be limited
          if no suitable license is detected.
          See the <a href="/license-policy">license policy</a> for more information.
        </p>

        <h2 id="feedback">Feedback</h2>
        <p>
          Share ideas, feature requests and bugs on the
          <a href="https://github.com/GlazKrovi/pkgsite/issues">issue tracker</a>.
          For the language itself, see <a href="https://github.com/GlazKrovi/lyuba/issues">Lyuba’s issues</a>.
        </p>

      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
{{end}}
